Expose a page-global media output volume on the glib WebKitWebView API.

WebKit already has a page-level media volume: WebCore Page::setMediaVolume(float)
is folded into HTMLMediaElement::effectiveVolume() as

    effectiveVolume = element.volume * page->mediaVolume() * ...

and pushed to the GStreamer player via player->setVolume(effectiveVolume()). It
is a true global attenuation multiplier applied to *every* media element in the
page, independent of the page-visible `.volume` attribute, and it is re-applied
automatically to media elements created later (effectiveVolume reads it live).
The full UIProcess->WebProcess plumbing exists (WKPageSetMediaVolume ->
WebPageProxy::setMediaVolume -> WebPage::SetMediaVolume -> Page::setMediaVolume,
and it is carried in the page creation parameters so it persists across loads),
but it is not surfaced on the public glib (WebKitWebView) binding that the
Atlantic Qt5 bridge uses.

Atlantic previously mirrored the system media volume by injecting JS to set
el.volume on each <audio>/<video>. Because that is the same property the site's
own player manages, players that drive their own el.volume (e.g. YouTube) fought
or reset it, so the system volume "didn't work". Setting the page media volume
instead is a separate multiplier the page JS cannot see or reset, applied to
every media element regardless of whether the site exposes a volume control —
a reliable browser-global volume. (Like el.volume, it covers HTMLMediaElement
output, i.e. <audio>/<video>; Web Audio graphs are a separate path either way.)

Mirrors the existing webkit_web_view_set_is_muted() shape (getPage().setMuted()).
--- a/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/API/glib/WebKitWebView.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/API/glib/WebKitWebView.cpp
@@ -3754,6 +3754,33 @@
 }
 
 /**
+ * webkit_web_view_set_media_volume:
+ * @web_view: a #WebKitWebView
+ * @volume: output volume in the [0, 1] range
+ *
+ * Sets a page-global output volume multiplier applied to every media element in
+ * @web_view, on top of each element's own `volume` attribute. This is the
+ * engine-level equivalent of a browser volume knob: it attenuates all audio and
+ * video playback in the page regardless of whether the site exposes its own
+ * volume control, and it does not modify the page-visible `.volume` of any media
+ * element. New media elements pick the value up automatically. Atlantic uses it
+ * to mirror the system media volume onto the page.
+ *
+ * Values outside [0, 1] are clamped.
+ */
+void webkit_web_view_set_media_volume(WebKitWebView* webView, gdouble volume)
+{
+    g_return_if_fail(WEBKIT_IS_WEB_VIEW(webView));
+
+    if (volume < 0)
+        volume = 0;
+    else if (volume > 1)
+        volume = 1;
+
+    getPage(webView).setMediaVolume(static_cast<float>(volume));
+}
+
+/**
  * webkit_web_view_go_back:
  * @web_view: a #WebKitWebView
  *
--- a/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/API/glib/WebKitWebView.h.in
